{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1513546-how-to-get-round-monocultural-dark-ages-mage-traditions","id":1513546,"title":"How to Get Round Monocultural Dark Ages Mage Traditions","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","World of Darkness","Mage: The Ascension"],"posts":[{"date":"2023-06-01T11:16:47-07:00","id":1513546,"title":"How to Get Round Monocultural Dark Ages Mage Traditions","content":"I'm gearing up for my first Dark Ages Mage game as a player, and I'm scratching my head a bit about how they're presented.<br /> <br /> Most Dark Age Traditions, apart from the Spirit-Talkers and arguably the Order of Hermes, has an attached cultural backdrop of some sort, rather than necessarily just being an archetype. As a result, I feel like it's a bit odd to not be attached to that particular culture. For example, being an Ahl-i-Batin who's not a Muslim, or a Valdaerman* who's not from northern Europe doesn't feel like it makes sense.<br /> <br /> I don't know the location of the game yet, but if it's something like Italy, it feels like it cuts out a good number of the Traditions purely on the basis of where the cultures that house those Traditions are based. Is there a way to conceptualise the Traditions given in Dark Ages without attaching them to a &quot;home culture&quot;? Or am I looking at this from totally the wrong angle and need to think about it in another way?","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"http://wondrousatlas.wordpress.com\" target=\"_blank\">Wondrous Atlas of Creation's Destiny</a> - I cohost an Exalted podcast focusing on examining the setting's lore and how to develop them into elements to use in games.<br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/924488-homebuilt-exalted-st-screen-reference-sheet\" target=\"_blank\">Ex3 Reference Materials</a> - currently includes an ST screen, common actions sheet, weapons reference sheet, character creation summary and mortal QCs reference sheet.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/497-xerxes","name":"Xerxes","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar497_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"557"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-06-01T12:57:29-07:00","id":1513562,"title":"","content":"The Old Faith isn't particularly rooted in one culture; nor are the Messianic Voices: both can be found all over Europe. As you say, the same can be said of the Order of Hermes and the Spirit Talkers. The only ones that are problematic in that regard are the Ahl-i-Batin and the Valdaermen, who are focused primarily in the Middle East and northern Europe respectively. In those two cases, you can resolve the issue by coming up with an outsider character concept: for instance, a Batini who has found himself in Valdaermen territory (see <i>The Thirteenth Warrior</i>\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b for an example of this).","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div style=\"margin-left:40px\"> <div align=\"center\">My hacks: <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwtrin\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#0000FF\">Trinity Continuum</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwscion\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#008000\">Scion</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwExalt\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#DAA520\">Exalted</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwcwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#800000\">World of Darkness</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwnwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#FF0000\">Chronicles of Darkness</span></a></div> </div>","edited":{"userid":421,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/421-dataweaver","user":"Dataweaver","date":". 06-05-2023, 06:54 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/421-dataweaver","name":"Dataweaver","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar421_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"8420"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-06-01T22:08:08-07:00","id":1513605,"title":"","content":"It's hard to say. (I have never really liked DA: Mage's set up because it always felt limited, both in the way you mention and others.) I suspect part of it is having to very carefully pick your settings. Constantinople might work OK (especially if you do it pre-Fourth Crusade), with Hermetics, Orthodox Messianics, Varingian Valdaermen, Batini (who may be Orthodox or Jewish rather than Muslim), Spirit Talkers, and maybe Greek influenced Old Faith. Places like the Napals medical college or Venice might also manage to have three or four groups fit in without feeling out of place. <br /> <br /> With the Ahl i Batan, I think the best bet is to decide that a lot of their European members are Sephardic Jews, which puts them in places like Italy, France, and London. (There's probably also Central/Eastern European Jews.)<br /> <br /> The Valdaermen probably have small numbers existing in secrecy in places like the Black Forest, the former parts of the Danelaw in England, the northern Scottish islands, Dublin, Normandy, and Constantinople.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i>What is tolerance? It is the consequence of humanity. We are all formed of frailty and error; let us pardon reciprocally each other's folly. That is the first law of nature.</i><br /> Voltaire, &quot;Tolerance&quot; (1764)","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12890-no-one-of-consequence","name":"No One of Consequence","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12890_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3937"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-06-01T23:21:58-07:00","id":1513607,"title":"","content":"I think we're underselling just how far-traveled the &quot;vikings&quot; were. They found their way as far as the Black Sea, hob-nobbing with the Byzantines as No One of Consequence said. It would be weird for them NOT to at least have the opportunity to show up anywhere in the vicinity of Europe. So long as there's a sea or even river route their long boats could take.<br /> <br /> Moreover, while I'm not well versed in the timeline of Scandinavian history, it's not implausible for a Valderman to be (or at least pretend to be) a Christianized Nordic person. At least for the purposes of interacting with most parts of Christianized Europe, without being discounted as an obvious pagan.<br /> <br /> As for the Al-i-Batin, we may be forgetting Al-Andalus. The Iberian region was controlled, whole or in part, by various Muslim dynasties from 711 to 1492. Meaning the &quot;Dark Ages&quot; setting of WoD, or its typical starting year, sits smack in the middle of that. So having Muslim (or Jewish) Batini in western Europe is very explainable.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div align=\"center\"><a href=\"http://archiveofourown.org/works/6956863/chapters/15862327\" target=\"_blank\">Magestuck: The Ascension</a></div>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6156-bluecho","name":"Bluecho","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar6156_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3871"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-06-02T01:03:16-07:00","id":1513618,"title":"","content":"It's a feature, not a flaw. Rather than 10+ poorly detailed traditions you can instead richly detail 3 or 4.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/25794-mywifeisscary","name":"MyWifeIsScary","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar25794_2.png","title":"Banned","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2019"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3711"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-06-04T14:03:26-07:00","id":1513892,"title":"","content":"The norse weren't particularly Xenophobic, and they traveled pretty much all over the world. So it wouldn't be that weird to learn that a Valdaerman had taken an apprentice from somewhere else, particularly since they aren't even particularly well liked in their homeland. The norse in general had an odd tendency to wander somewhere else and then just dissolve into the local culture. See the Kievan Rus, or the Normans, or the Gallowglass for examples.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11744-enginseer-42","name":"Enginseer-42","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"774"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-06-05T00:10:12-07:00","id":1513923,"title":"","content":"I don't have DA: Mage handy to double check their official history and such, but for the sake of argument, let's presume that the Valdaermen go back at least to Roman empire era Germania and its traditions of seeresses, necromancers, and rune masters, and have at least a small presence among the barbarian migrations at the end of the western empire, perhaps even still in the Carolingian empire. And also a much stronger presence with the Anglo, Saxon, and other groups moving into post Roman Britain. And then obviously with the Viking era Danes and Norse.<br /> So, from that, Places You Might Find Valdaermen :<ul><li>Scandinavia</li> <li>The British Isles, including Ireland (Dublin especially)</li> <li>Germany, especially around the Rhine, Saxony, and the Black Forest, as well as the border marches with Poland</li> <li>The Kievan Rus towns</li> <li>Constantinople and other trading ports of the Byzantine Empire, including the Black Sea and Adriatic (maybe even Venice)</li> <li>Norman lands including northern France and southern Italy</li> <li>Trading ports in Iberia and the Mediterranean</li> </ul><br /> Obviously, a lot of these places aren't going to have very many of them, and they likely keep pretty undercover, but I think you can still justify one or three of them being there.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i>What is tolerance? It is the consequence of humanity. We are all formed of frailty and error; let us pardon reciprocally each other's folly. That is the first law of nature.</i><br /> Voltaire, &quot;Tolerance&quot; (1764)","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12890-no-one-of-consequence","name":"No One of Consequence","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12890_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3937"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-06-05T03:28:08-07:00","id":1513928,"title":"","content":"Don't worry too much about that. Mages of all backgrounds travelling to strange new lands is basically how the traditions formed.<br /> <br /> For exemple, the two founders of the verbena, a british witch and a vaelderman, both went across the world to call mages to go to misdridge, she went to the americas then across africa, and he went across the russian peninsula and met the akashic before disappearing.<br /> <br /> Same for the batini, whose doctrine of unity made them travel the world. <br /> <br /> There are many reasons for a dark ages mage to travel, be it a mystic quest, vebgeance, seeking knowledge and so on.<br /> <br /> <br /> You could even link those quests to the place of your game with the local legends which are probably true in that era of high myths.<br /> <br /> For exemple, in italy, there's this lake whose source comes from a cavern, and the ancient roman though it was the entrance to the underworld. Which is probably true in the dark ages. So a vaeldermen could have heard of the legends, and decided to go there to save someone they love from hellheim.<br /> <br /> A batini could come alongside an hermetic to find ancient mystical grimoires kept in the vault of heretical texts in the vatican.<br /> <br /> <br /> \u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/24043-nyremne","name":"Nyremne","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2018"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"414"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-06-05T03:54:08-07:00","id":1513933,"title":"","content":"(my original post got held up by the spam filter; <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/frown.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Frown\" smilieid=\"11\" class=\"inlineimg\" /> so this is a \u201cbump\u201d to let people know it's there. Sorry about that. EDIT: never mind. I tried editing it to change \u201cnot\u201d to \u201cnor\u201d, and it got flagged again. I hate that spam filter.<br /> EDIT 2: hopefully they clear it soon. But in a nutshell, I was trying to point out <i>The Thirteenth Warrior</i>, where an Arab ended up in Norse territory.)","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div style=\"margin-left:40px\"> <div align=\"center\">My hacks: <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwtrin\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#0000FF\">Trinity Continuum</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwscion\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#008000\">Scion</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwExalt\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#DAA520\">Exalted</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwcwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#800000\">World of Darkness</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwnwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#FF0000\">Chronicles of Darkness</span></a></div> </div>","edited":{"userid":421,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/421-dataweaver","user":"Dataweaver","date":". 06-05-2023, 07:12 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/421-dataweaver","name":"Dataweaver","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar421_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"8420"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-06-05T04:09:40-07:00","id":1513938,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Nyremne</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1513546-how-to-get-round-monocultural-dark-ages-mage-traditions?p=1513928#post1513928\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Don't worry too much about that. Mages of all backgrounds travelling to strange new lands is basically how the traditions formed.\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b</div> </div> </div> </div>This. Ideas travel as fast as people (nowadays faster), and once established tend to stay. Even if a character isn't directly from the formative branch of a Tradition, there can easily exist a 'palette swap' that has been adapted to local culture- there is also parallel evolution of ideas, which means a Tradition branch could have developed independently but share most of the same core aspects. I have seen spells in Icelandic grimoires that look suspiciously similar to Arabian ones.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Custom Sorcery: <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1487084-pattern-weaving-ananasi-sorcery-homebrew\" target=\"_blank\">For Ananasi</a> | Mortal:<a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1509365-hekau-for-sorcerers\" target=\"_blank\"> Hekau</a>, <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1550552-ma%C5%A1ma%C5%A1%C5%A1%C5%ABtu-mesopotamian-sorcery\" target=\"_blank\">Ma\u0161ma\u0161\u0161\u016btu</a> | Necromancy:<a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1513911-have-you-your-players-ever-used-disciplines-sorcery-for-scholarly-pursuits/page2#post1514352\" target=\"_blank\"> Dust Path</a>, <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1535508-dav20-older-lamia-lore?p=1536244#post1536244\" target=\"_blank\">P.o.Fertile Grave Soil</a> | Thauma: <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1513911-have-you-your-players-ever-used-disciplines-sorcery-for-scholarly-pursuits/page2#post1515316\" target=\"_blank\">Ars Notoria</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/27477-voidshaper","name":"voidshaper","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar27477_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2022"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"698"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-06-05T05:25:42-07:00","id":1513941,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Dataweaver</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1513546-how-to-get-round-monocultural-dark-ages-mage-traditions?p=1513933#post1513933\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">EDIT 2: hopefully they clear it soon. But in a nutshell, I was trying to point out <i>The Thirteenth Warrior</i>, where an Arab ended up in Norse territory.)</div> </div> </div> </div>I will note that Michael Crichton's novel was inspired by <i>Ibn Fadlan and the Land of Darkness</i>, a real 10th century account of an Arab travelling up the Volga into the Kievan Rus and his encounters with Norse traders and adventurers. There actually a number of fascinating medieval travelogues that have survived, including some late Norse ones.<br /> <br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i>What is tolerance? It is the consequence of humanity. We are all formed of frailty and error; let us pardon reciprocally each other's folly. That is the first law of nature.</i><br /> Voltaire, &quot;Tolerance&quot; (1764)","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12890-no-one-of-consequence","name":"No One of Consequence","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12890_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3937"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-06-06T08:14:05-07:00","id":1514015,"title":"","content":"Well I tend to see the entrenched well defined groups as Infinatly preferable the homogenous archeotypes. But to answer your query the old faith are noted to be present on the outskirts of most ethnic groups and peoples.<br /> <br /> The valdermann is quite easy to circumvent by shifting to germanic rather than norse. You could easily establish them as present on a wider scale prior to charlamaigine and the christianisation of the Anglo saxons.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":26986,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/26986-ragged-robin","user":"Ragged Robin","date":". 06-06-2023, 07:00 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/26986-ragged-robin","name":"Ragged Robin","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar26986_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2021"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1720"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-06-09T16:08:34-07:00","id":1514350,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Xerxes</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1513546-how-to-get-round-monocultural-dark-ages-mage-traditions#post1513546\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I'm gearing up for my first Dark Ages Mage game as a player, and I'm scratching my head a bit about how they're presented.<br /> <br /> Most Dark Age Traditions, apart from the Spirit-Talkers and arguably the Order of Hermes, has an attached cultural backdrop of some sort, rather than necessarily just being an archetype. As a result, I feel like it's a bit odd to not be attached to that particular culture. For example, being an Ahl-i-Batin who's not a Muslim, or a Valdaerman* who's not from northern Europe doesn't feel like it makes sense.<br /> <br /> I don't know the location of the game yet, but if it's something like Italy, it feels like it cuts out a good number of the Traditions purely on the basis of where the cultures that house those Traditions are based. Is there a way to conceptualise the Traditions given in Dark Ages without attaching them to a &quot;home culture&quot;? Or am I looking at this from totally the wrong angle and need to think about it in another way?</div> </div> </div> </div>It can be where ever you want it to be; a captured slave from a Viking-raid could learn Valdaerman's art and same goes with an captured slave of Ahl-i-Batin. The more implausible to the setting, the more create of an explanation you'll have to come up with. <br /> <br /> And mages do like to travel. <br /> <br /> Me, I would just make up a fictional-fief as most of the action will take place there and not worry about country, just abstractly. Yes, it can take place in France, but, Northern-France and Souther-France are different; they even spoke a different version of French.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>&quot;I have all the power in the world to give you, and all you have to do is beg&quot; -Nix<br /> <br /> &quot;There's thing about being a vampire, Frank; it takes a long fucking time to learn how do all the cool shit&quot;. -Abigail","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2521-iguazu","name":"Iguazu","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2521_3.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"740"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-06-12T12:41:56-07:00","id":1514738,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Xerxes</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1513546-how-to-get-round-monocultural-dark-ages-mage-traditions#post1513546\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I'm gearing up for my first Dark Ages Mage game as a player, and I'm scratching my head a bit about how they're presented.</div> </div> </div> </div>People travelled a lot even in medieval times. Especially Magi. The Old Faith, Spirit Talkers and Order of Hermes are everywhere. The first two are going to appear wherever you have folkloric wisewomen/men, shamans, paganic cults. The Old Faith can as easily be a Etruscan or Roman cult in Italy as it could be Fae followers in Spain or the Harvest priests of Kievan Rus. The Spirit Talkers can be sin-eaters, intercessors between the dark woods and the village or village madmen. OoH has an academic strucutre and outposts and scholars all across the known world, taking in many practitioners with a similar approach to magic.<br /> <br /> Vaelderman and Ahl-i-Batini are the two that are more complicated, but they are also two of the most travelled peoples in medieval times. Spain, North Africa, Turkey and the Middle East, India and China are all stomping grounds for the folk where the Batini originated, but these folks travel all over the world. A Vizier would have even more reasons to travel than the average man. And considering their approach is as spies, infiltrators and subtle manipulators, they would easily recruit people and found secret headquarters and places where they can keep an eye on the comings and goings of the world.<br /> <br /> Varlderman are also belonging to a well travelled people - after all nordic warriors raided Britain, Spain, Mediterranean and even worked as mercenaries for the Eastern Roman Empire. There are many reasons for their high priests to follow them into adventure and fortune. Just like there are many reasons for them to leave home in disgust of how their on people are abandoning the true Gods and following the dead king of the cross.<br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"font-family:arial\">What doesn't kill you, makes you...<i> stranger</i>.</span>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":6}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1182-asmodai","name":"Asmodai","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1182_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2989"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-06-14T03:29:40-07:00","id":1514932,"title":"","content":"Yes, people travelled, sometimes, and wrote interesting travelogues. But outside of trade and port cities, this is more about exceptions than rules. Yes, mages are exceptional people, but just because you <i>can </i>doesn't mean you should. It seems like a bit of a modern perversion to force diversity where it doesn't belong, in my view that's cultural imperialism.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/25794-mywifeisscary","name":"MyWifeIsScary","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar25794_2.png","title":"Banned","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2019"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3711"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-06-14T04:26:48-07:00","id":1514937,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>MyWifeIsScary</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1513546-how-to-get-round-monocultural-dark-ages-mage-traditions?p=1514932#post1514932\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">It seems like a bit of a modern perversion to force diversity where it doesn't belong, in my view that's cultural imperialism.</div> </div> </div> </div>I don't think anybody is doing that. I would not describe providing an exceptional or unlikely background story for a culturally displaced character as imperialism, it's just a very common narrative tool. Stranger in a strange land is a classic trope, and it's the reason why those travelogues are so interesting. Contrasting or even clashing cultures isn't a problem as long as you do not override one culture with another.<br /> <br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Custom Sorcery: <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1487084-pattern-weaving-ananasi-sorcery-homebrew\" target=\"_blank\">For Ananasi</a> | Mortal:<a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1509365-hekau-for-sorcerers\" target=\"_blank\"> Hekau</a>, <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1550552-ma%C5%A1ma%C5%A1%C5%A1%C5%ABtu-mesopotamian-sorcery\" target=\"_blank\">Ma\u0161ma\u0161\u0161\u016btu</a> | Necromancy:<a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1513911-have-you-your-players-ever-used-disciplines-sorcery-for-scholarly-pursuits/page2#post1514352\" target=\"_blank\"> Dust Path</a>, <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1535508-dav20-older-lamia-lore?p=1536244#post1536244\" target=\"_blank\">P.o.Fertile Grave Soil</a> | Thauma: <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1513911-have-you-your-players-ever-used-disciplines-sorcery-for-scholarly-pursuits/page2#post1515316\" target=\"_blank\">Ars Notoria</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/27477-voidshaper","name":"voidshaper","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar27477_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2022"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"698"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-06-14T05:00:17-07:00","id":1514947,"title":"","content":"I'm coming from more <b>CoD</b> background - but if I remember my own <b>M:tAs</b> games I played - are mages not exceptional by default? They are de facto 'philosophical demigods'. Not counting things like Correspondance / Space Sphere in the first place. Travelling mages is a setting standard, not excetpion.<br /> <br /> In <b>M:tAw</b> talks with official devs it was pointed that Africa would be culturally joined to Orders by CENTURIES before white man colonisation from late medivial period.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1437-wyrdhamster","name":"wyrdhamster","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1437_5.jpg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"11313"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-06-16T18:01:05-07:00","id":1515320,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>wyrdhamster</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1513546-how-to-get-round-monocultural-dark-ages-mage-traditions?p=1514947#post1514947\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I'm coming from more <b>CoD</b> background - but if I remember my own <b>M:tAs</b> games I played - are mages not exceptional by default? They are de facto 'philosophical demigods'. Not counting things like Correspondance / Space Sphere in the first place. Travelling mages is a setting standard, not excetpion.<br /> <br /> In <b>M:tAw</b> talks with official devs it was pointed that Africa would be culturally joined to Orders by CENTURIES before white man colonisation from late medivial period.</div> </div> </div> </div>Kinda exceptional; I think they got more points (five for spheres and seven for backgrounds), because they were a bit weaker than the traditional WoD characters (Vampires and Garou).","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>&quot;I have all the power in the world to give you, and all you have to do is beg&quot; -Nix<br /> <br /> &quot;There's thing about being a vampire, Frank; it takes a long fucking time to learn how do all the cool shit&quot;. -Abigail","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2521-iguazu","name":"Iguazu","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2521_3.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"740"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-06-16T20:35:37-07:00","id":1515325,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>voidshaper</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1513546-how-to-get-round-monocultural-dark-ages-mage-traditions?p=1514937#post1514937\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Stranger in a strange land is a classic trope,</div> </div> </div> </div>While this is true, if four out of five PCs are travellers from an external culture, you really don\u2019t wind up doing stranger in a strange land any more. The stranger character is a good narrative device because it provides the point of view for an audience who is not of the featured culture to learn about that culture, and gives the other characters a reason to show and explain aspects of their culture. When most main characters are each of a different culture, you wind up focussing a lot more on the differences between PC cultures.<br /> <br /> It seems to me that, rather than touching lightly on four or five different cultures, a Dark Ages game is a great opportunity to do a deep dive into one culture. Instead of hunting for excuses to pull in multiple other Traditions, expand the Tradition of the featured culture so that there is a variety of interesting niches for players to choose. When you don\u2019t have Verbena hoovering up all the Life mages, the Order of Hermes doing the same for Forces mages, and so on, the single dominant Tradition of the culture should naturally expand - not necessarily to the point of becoming a monocultural Nine Traditions Council, but certainly such that there are multiple options for Affinity Sphere within the one Tradition. And even within Sphere, there can be groups with different Sphere specialties and rotes, different practices.<br /> <br /> Also, if you choose to set your game in a time and place of turmoil, you can easily have two clashing cultures. There may be war between them, or it may be more of a slow burn - new settlers, intermarriage, trade. But there\u2019s always fringes where cultures meet.<br /> <br /> And then, there\u2019s still the option of an out-of-place character, serving as your stranger in a strange land.<br /> <br /> So if you\u2019ve got 5 players, you might have 5 from one culture (and expanded options to provide niches); or 4 plus a stranger; a mix from two local culture; or 2 and 2 from competing local cultures plus a stranger.<br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11151-travisjhall","name":"travisjhall","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"109"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-06-17T01:55:51-07:00","id":1515337,"title":"","content":"All Player Characters are, by definition, exceptional. They are Main Characters, made special by dint of having a player controlling them and being the center of a Storyteller's campaign. Even if they weren't also Mages, PCs would have license to be whatever exception you wanted. If a player wanted their PC to come from a different locale, that's their right. They're &quot;allowed&quot; to break normal rules.<br /> <br /> Not, as we've established, that it's unusual for a person to be well traveled in the middle ages. They do it all the time. The idea of homogenous populations that never interact with one another is a myth created by our own modern, reductive view of history, informed by equally reductive historical narratives. People moved around. Traders traded, mercenaries sought work, people got exiled, folks deserted from armies marching to foreign shores. People <i>went on vacation</i>. Even peasants might travel from village to village as part of pilgrimage. (Heck, you could do a lot with the idea of characters rushing off to the Crusades and returning having been turned to the ways of the Ahl-i-Batin.)<br /> <br /> In a game all about Magick and pushing against what's possible, taking issue with &quot;person in one location using magick style associated with another location&quot; seems to go against the spirit of the game. You're already playing people who can turn flower petals into pegasi. You can suspend your precious disbelief about a Viking adventuring in central France.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div align=\"center\"><a href=\"http://archiveofourown.org/works/6956863/chapters/15862327\" target=\"_blank\">Magestuck: The Ascension</a></div>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6156-bluecho","name":"Bluecho","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar6156_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3871"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-06-17T06:28:23-07:00","id":1515356,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Bluecho</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1513546-how-to-get-round-monocultural-dark-ages-mage-traditions?p=1515337#post1515337\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">If a player wanted their PC to come from a different locale, that's their right.</div> </div> </div> </div>No, it\u2019s not. It\u2019s a game played by the mutual consent of the participants. There\u2019s no rights beyond what the group agrees to.<br /> <br /> There\u2019s not necessarily any problem with playing MDA using characters from all over the world, but it\u2019s also perfectly okay to put together a game which features exploration of one culture, or a small set of cultures. If there\u2019s one player who doesn\u2019t want to do that, nobody is going to force them to participate.<br /> <br /> Also, it\u2019s pretty common for GMs to propose a game in which characters should fit certain requirements. It\u2019s not outlandish to ask for only Mystics, for example. (It was once the default mode of play for M:tA.) Asking for characters from particular cultures isn\u2019t much different.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":11151,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11151-travisjhall","user":"travisjhall","date":". 06-17-2023, 09:34 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11151-travisjhall","name":"travisjhall","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"109"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-06-18T05:15:36-07:00","id":1515453,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>MyWifeIsScary</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1513546-how-to-get-round-monocultural-dark-ages-mage-traditions?p=1514932#post1514932\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Yes, people travelled, sometimes, and wrote interesting travelogues. But outside of trade and port cities, this is more about exceptions than rules. Yes, mages are exceptional people, but just because you <i>can </i>doesn't mean you should. It seems like a bit of a modern perversion to force diversity where it doesn't belong, in my view that's cultural imperialism.</div> </div> </div> </div>I think you're soapboxing here. Just because the majority of the populations weren't mobile doesn't mean a huge number of people were not mobile. And this pool was whole orders of magnitude larger than the number of Mages in the era of DA:Mage.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"font-family:arial\">What doesn't kill you, makes you...<i> stranger</i>.</span>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1182-asmodai","name":"Asmodai","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1182_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2989"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-07-30T10:04:06-07:00","id":1519178,"title":"","content":"Historically is can vary region to region and personal circumstances People certainly travelled less than today for a number of reasons<br /> <br /> If i want cosmopolitan games I tend to run in places like the byzantine empire or the uybassid caliphate. Pretty much anything along the Silk Road is easy to justify but an Akashic running around Ireland or a valderman in the empty quarter is going to need explaining.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":26986,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/26986-ragged-robin","user":"Ragged Robin","date":". 07-31-2023, 10:20 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/26986-ragged-robin","name":"Ragged Robin","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar26986_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2021"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1720"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-07-31T18:57:34-07:00","id":1519306,"title":"","content":"The first thing to remember is that DA Mage, even less than other DA lines or regular modern Mage, is't a game where the ST makes whatever campaign and the players make whatever characters and you just kind of play. The ST and players need to coordinate at least somewhat.<br /> <br /> As has been pointed out, there are plenty of ways to justify Mages who are away from their home culture, but they all need to consider the context of the story,","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/456-blackwell","name":"Blackwell","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar456_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"977"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-08-03T09:44:27-07:00","id":1519636,"title":"","content":"So, I read that, there are Followers of Set vamps in Scandinavia in the D<i>ark Ages</i>, so why not? <br /> <br /> This may not be relevant to the O.P.'s question, but, the <i>Ars Magic</i>a books are exceptionally good. The book about viking goes into details about rune-magic, which, I mostly glossed over as I don't fully understand <i>Ars Magica</i>'s magic-system and lots, and lots of details of Viking-culture. <br /> <br /> The <i>Order of Hermes</i> Book, for <i>Ars Magica</i>, goes into detail about a the magi-order in the Middle-Ages and it's a good supplement for <i>Dark Ages: Mage</i>. In order doesn't seem to have a specific-culture in mind, other than European.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>&quot;I have all the power in the world to give you, and all you have to do is beg&quot; -Nix<br /> <br /> &quot;There's thing about being a vampire, Frank; it takes a long fucking time to learn how do all the cool shit&quot;. -Abigail","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2521-iguazu","name":"Iguazu","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2521_3.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"740"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-08-03T16:56:39-07:00","id":1519680,"title":"","content":"[QUOTE=Bluecho;n1515337]<br /> (...)<br /> People moved around. Traders traded, mercenaries sought work, people got exiled, folks deserted from armies marching to foreign shores. People <i>went on vacation</i>. Even peasants might travel from village to village as part of pilgrimage. (Heck, you could do a lot with the idea of characters rushing off to the Crusades and returning having been turned to the ways of the Ahl-i-Batin.)<br /> /QUOTE]<br /> Religious and faith travel, pilgrimages, and migrations - other than the Crusades - are definitely worth mentioning.<br /> Forest, mountainous, riverside, and roadside shrines and temples were tended to, financed, regularly renovated, and defnitiely respected. Religious gatherings and celebrations provided another incentive to both travel, and also to welcome and trust tourists, visitors, pilgrims, and travellers. <br /> <br /> Other categories of wanderers and travellers in the Dark Ages include royal, aristocratic, and noble exiles and expatriates. There is also the interesting, definite, and clear possibility of scouts, pioneers, and explorers.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3254-muad-dib","name":"Muad'Dib","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3254_3.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2059"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}